      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-166613",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/80/80fb15f8be9d3e202ebabebf19771ccfeb24b77754e30fcf2e1d6141f5d6f8e4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a rational function. It has no vertical asymptotes, its $x$-intercepts are $(1, 0)$ and $(2, 0)$, and its $y$--intercept is $(0, 1)$. Which of the following could be the equation of the graph?\n\n1. $y = (2(x - 1)(x - 2))/(4(x^{2} + 1))$\n2. $y = ((x + 1)(x - 2))/(4(x^{2} + 1))$\n3. $y = ((x - 1)(x - 2))/(4(x + 1))$\n4. $y = ((x - 1)(x - 2))/(x^{2} + 4)$\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*"
